{
  "id": "thunderbird",
  "name": "Mozilla Thunderbird",
  "kind": "app",
  "category": "Communication",
  "match": {
    "windows_exe": ["thunderbird.exe"],
    "macos_bundle": ["org.mozilla.thunderbird"],
    "window_title": []
  },
  "sources": [
    "https://support.mozilla.org/en-US/kb/keyboard-shortcuts-thunderbird"
  ],
  "note": "From the official Mozilla Support 'Keyboard shortcuts' article for Thunderbird (last updated 2026-04-02). The source states the shortcut modifier is Ctrl on Windows/Linux and Cmd (Command) on macOS, and gives no separate Linux table, so 'linux' is omitted everywhere (equals windows) and 'mac' is derived from that Ctrl->Cmd rule for every Ctrl-based chord. The source never documents Mac equivalents for its Alt-based chords (Option-key combos on macOS commonly produce accented characters instead), so 'mac' is omitted for all Alt-prefixed shortcuts rather than guessed. Skipped as mouse-only: starting a message in a non-default format (Shift+click a toolbar button), opening a message/folder in a background or foreground tab (middle-click / Shift+middle-click), selecting multiple adjacent/non-adjacent messages (Shift+click / Ctrl+click), copying or moving a message to another folder (drag / Ctrl+drag), and selecting a table cell/rows/columns/table while composing (Ctrl+click / drag). Skipped as generic numbered patterns rather than one fixed chord (same precedent as other catalog entries): 'Add/Remove Tag' (1-9) and switching directly to compose/mail Tab 2 through 8 (Ctrl+2 .. Ctrl+8); Tab 1 (Ctrl+1) and the Last Tab (Ctrl+9) are still included since those are single fixed chords. Skipped as not expressible in this grammar: 'Expand All Threads' (a bare '*', historically the numpad multiply key, not in the allowed key set), the Menu-key variant of 'Show context menu' (no standard name for that key; the Shift+F10 variant is kept), the bare 'Alt' press of 'Toggle Menu Bar' (a modifier alone has no non-modifier key; the F10 variant is kept), the Ctrl+> / Ctrl+< variants of Larger/Smaller Font Size ('>' and '<' are not in the allowed punctuation set; the equivalent Ctrl+. / Ctrl+, forms are used instead), and the Ctrl+scroll-wheel variants of Zoom In/Out. 'Show Events Filter bar' (Calendar tab) was left out because its shortcut cell was garbled/illegible in the source page. 'Quit Thunderbird' is documented only as the File > Exit menu item, with no keyboard shortcut. 'Hide Thunderbird' and 'Hide Other Windows' are noted as Mac-only menu items with no chord given. Alt+F4 (close window), Ctrl+F4 (close tab) and Win+Down (minimize) are kept Windows-only as documented, standard window-manager shortcuts with no stated Mac equivalent.",
